src/test/modules/test_checksums/spec/README

A formal model of the data checksum state machine
=================================================
This directory contains a machine-checked model, written in Lean 4, of the code
that reads and modifies the data checksum state of a cluster.  It is a
specification, not a test: nothing here is built or run by "make check" or by
meson, and no PostgreSQL source file depends on it.

The informal specification it formalizes is the header comment of
src/backend/postmaster/datachecksum_state.c, in particular the two rules

    all backends MUST calculate and write data checksums during all states
    except off; MUST validate checksums only in the 'on' state.

and the claim that the two intermediate states plus the procsignal barrier
handshake are enough to make those rules hold while the state is changing
underneath running backends.


What is proved
==============
Two properties, for every reachable cluster state, for an arbitrary number of
backends and an arbitrary number of pages (Spec/Safety.lean):

  reachable_writeSafe
      If any process verifies data checksums, then every process is writing
      them.  This is the first rule above, stated over the readers
      DataChecksumsNeedVerify() and DataChecksumsNeedWrite() rather than over
      the states themselves.

  reachable_readSafe
      If any process verifies data checksums, then every page on disk carries
      one.

  no_spurious_failure
      The corollary the feature is really about: PageIsVerified() never reports
      a checksum failure on a page the cluster itself wrote.

Alongside those, a handful of finite audits of the checksum_barriers table are
proved by evaluation, and are the first thing to look at if that table is ever
edited:

  barrierTable_length             the table still has nine entries
  barrierTable_mixSafe            no entry pairs a verifying backend with a
                                  non-writing one
  barrierTable_no_direct_toggle   there is no direct on/off edge either way
  barrierTable_pairwiseSafe       the full 4^3 case check behind write safety
  coordinatorEdges_absorbable     every transition the launcher and xlog2_redo()
                                  drive is accepted by
                                  AbsorbDataChecksumsBarrier(), so no barrier
                                  can livelock on the ereport(ERROR) retry path

Three negative results show the audits have teeth, i.e. that they would fail if
the design were weakened: onOff_shortcut_unsafe, offOn_shortcut_unsafe and
twoStateDesign_unsafe.  The last one is the formal statement of why the two
'inprogress-*' states have to exist at all.

Finally, Spec/Trace.lean exhibits a concrete nine-step execution of a
two-backend cluster that performs a complete online enable, so the results above
are not vacuous.  Its state c8 is the situation the whole design exists to
survive: one backend has absorbed the barrier and verifies checksums while
another has not and merely writes them.


What is modelled
================
The model covers the three copies of the state and every function that reads or
writes them.

  Lean (Spec/)                     C
  -------------------------------  ------------------------------------------
  ChecksumState                    ChecksumStateType (storage/checksum.h)
  ChecksumState.toVersion          PG_DATA_CHECKSUM_* encoding
  Cluster.shared                   XLogCtl->data_checksum_version
  Cluster.control                  ControlFile->data_checksum_version
  Cluster.localState               LocalDataChecksumState, one per process
  Cluster.onDisk                   whether the page on disk carries a checksum

  needWrite                        DataChecksumsNeedWrite()
  needVerify                       DataChecksumsNeedVerify()
                                   DataChecksumsOn/Off/InProgressOn() are
                                   definable from Cluster.shared and are not
                                   given names of their own

  barrierTable                     checksum_barriers[9]
  absorbAllowed                    the validation in
                                   AbsorbDataChecksumsBarrier()
  coordinatorEdges                 the transitions SetDataChecksums*() and
                                   xlog2_redo() drive
  recover                          the end-of-recovery fixups in StartupXLOG()

  Step.backendStart                InitLocalDataChecksumState()
  Step.absorb                      AbsorbDataChecksumsBarrier()
  Step.writePage                   PageSetChecksum(), and by extension the
                                   XLogHintBitIsNeeded() paths, and the
                                   datachecksums worker rewriting a page
  Step.advanceShared               the XLogCtl assignment under info_lck in
                                   SetDataChecksumsOnInProgress(),
                                   SetDataChecksumsOn(), SetDataChecksumsOff()
                                   and xlog2_redo()
  Step.advanceControl              ControlFile assignment plus
                                   UpdateControlFile() under ControlFileLock
  Step.restart                     crash or immediate shutdown, then
                                   XLOGShmemInit() plus StartupXLOG()
  Init                             initdb and offline pg_checksums

Reads are deliberately not steps.  Every consumer of the state -- PageIsVerified(),
PageSetChecksum(), XLogHintBitIsNeeded() and its callers in bufmgr.c,
pruneheap.c, visibilitymap.c and freespace.c, backup_checksums_verifiable(),
show_data_checksums() -- is a pure function of one process' local copy, so "a
read misbehaves" is a property of a state rather than of a transition.  That is
what makes the safety statements above say everything there is to say about the
readers.

Splitting advanceShared from advanceControl is what exposes the window that
SetDataChecksumsOn() reasons about in its comment:

    Update the controlfile before waiting since if we have an immediate
    shutdown while waiting we want to come back up with checksums enabled.

A Step.restart may happen in that window, and the invariant fields
controlOnShared, controlOnWrites and controlOnPages are exactly what is needed
to show the cluster comes back up safely.

The key insight of the proof is that the obvious invariant -- "every local state
is barrier-compatible with the shared state" -- is too weak.  It admits a
population where one backend is at 'on' and another at 'off', which is precisely
what must not happen.  The extra fact needed is Inv.atMostTwo: the processes
that have not caught up all sit on the same previous state, because a new edge
is only ever started from a quiescent cluster.  That is what
WaitForProcSignalBarrier() buys.


Assumptions
===========
These are the places where the model is weaker than reality, and where a bug
could therefore hide.

* One coordinator at a time.  Step.advanceShared requires the cluster to be
  quiescent, which encodes both the WaitForProcSignalBarrier() at the end of
  each SetDataChecksums*() call and the fact that only one process drives
  transitions: the launcher is a singleton (DataChecksumsState->launcher_running)
  on a primary, and the startup process is the only replayer on a standby.

* Barriers are absorbed atomically.  The model has no notion of a backend
  observing shared memory part-way through a writer's update sequence.  This is
  justified by info_lck and by the fact that backends only ever read their own
  local copy.

* AbsorbDataChecksumsBarrier() skips validation entirely when
  RecoveryInProgress().  The model always applies the table.  Standby safety
  therefore rests on the primary's edge sequence being one the table would have
  accepted, which is what coordinatorEdges_absorbable checks.

* WAL is not modelled.  A standby is abstracted as replaying the primary's
  sequence of edges, so xlog2_redo() and the XLOG_CHECKPOINT_REDO handling
  appear only as coordinator transitions.  In particular
  GetLastChecksumChangeRecPtr() and backup_checksums_verifiable() are out of
  scope: they are about whether a base backup taken across a transition can be
  verified, which is an LSN-ordering question, not a state-machine one.

* Liveness is out of scope.  The model says nothing about the datachecksums
  worker making progress, about the ereport(ERROR) retry loop in
  AbsorbDataChecksumsBarrier() terminating, or about the cost-delay throttling.
  Only safety is proved.

* Pages are abstracted to a single bit, "carries a valid checksum".  Torn pages,
  the pd_checksum field itself and the checksum algorithm are not modelled.


Building
========
The model is written in plain Lean 4 with no dependencies -- in particular it
does not use Mathlib -- so it builds offline and checks in a couple of seconds:

    cd src/test/modules/test_checksums/spec
    lake build

lean-toolchain pins the exact compiler version, which is what the standard Lean
version manager, elan, reads:

    curl https://elan.lean-lang.org/elan-init.sh -sSf | sh

Alternatively, download the release named in lean-toolchain from
https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/releases and put its bin directory on PATH.

A successful "lake build" is the whole verification: there are no tests to run.
The development contains no "sorry" and no added axioms; the top-level results
depend only on propext, which can be confirmed with

    #print axioms Spec.reachable_readSafe


Why Lean, and what would be a better fit
========================================
Lean was chosen because it was asked for, and it does deliver something no model
checker can: the theorems hold for an arbitrary number of backends and pages,
not for three backends and two pages.  reachable_writeSafe is quantified over
Backend and Page as opaque types, so there is no state explosion to bound and no
"we checked up to N" caveat.  Lean also lets the finite parts of the argument --
the barrier table audits -- be discharged by "decide", i.e. by exhaustive
evaluation inside the proof, so the two styles of reasoning live in one
artifact.  The negative results are a real benefit of that mix: it is easy to
state "adding this edge to the table breaks safety" and have the compiler check
it.

That said, TLA+ with the TLC model checker would be the more conventional choice
for this problem, and would complement rather than replace this development.
This is a concurrency problem about interleavings and about a handshake
protocol, which is what TLA+ was designed for, and the community that would have
to maintain such a spec is far more likely to have seen TLA+ than Lean.  More
concretely, TLC gives three things this development does not:

* Liveness.  The livelock risk in AbsorbDataChecksumsBarrier() -- a rejected
  edge raises an error, procsignal retries, and WaitForProcSignalBarrier() never
  returns -- is a temporal property.  Here it can only be approached indirectly,
  by proving that the coordinator never emits a rejected edge
  (coordinatorEdges_absorbable).  TLC would check the real property under
  fairness assumptions.

* Counterexample traces.  When an invariant fails, TLC prints the interleaving
  that breaks it.  A failing Lean proof prints an unsolved goal, which is much
  less useful for finding out what the code actually does wrong.

* Cost of change.  Extending this model to cover, say, the LSN ordering behind
  backup_checksums_verifiable() means new invariant fields and new proof
  obligations in every case of Inv.step.  In TLA+ it means adding a variable and
  re-running TLC.

Alloy would be a third option, well suited to the finite table audits in
Spec/Safety.lean, but weaker than either for the temporal parts.

The pragmatic recommendation, if this were to be developed further: keep the
Lean development for the unbounded safety argument and for the barrier table
audit, which is the part most likely to catch a future edit to
checksum_barriers, and add a small TLA+ spec of the same state machine to
model-check liveness and to produce traces.  The two agree on the state space,
so the TLA+ spec would be a direct transcription of Spec/Cluster.lean.


Files
=====
    Spec/State.lean     the four states and the reader API
    Spec/Barriers.lean  checksum_barriers, the coordinator edges, recovery
    Spec/Cluster.lean   the cluster and its transition relation
    Spec/Safety.lean    the table audits, the invariant, the safety theorems
    Spec/Trace.lean     a concrete execution, so the results are not vacuous
